                <pb facs="0923" n="891" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p1/pg891"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.13/12–153: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador at Saigon</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Heath</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Saigon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-12-01T19:00:00+08:00">December 1, 1953—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>942. Sent Paris 271. Bao Dai told me today that immediately after Prime Minister
                    Tam’s return Paris, a new government would be formed.</p>
                <pb facs="0924" n="892" xml:id="pg892"/>
                <p>Tam would be allowed to exit gracefully since Bao Dai would suggest that he take
                    a rest period, the intimation being that he would be recalled to government at a
                    later period. The new government would be a “temporary” government.</p>
                <p>This meant, Bao Dai said, that Tam would not be making the trip to the States to
                    which he had been invited by reason of his position as Prime Minister.</p>
                <p>Bao Dai did not indicate who would head or form the new government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Heath</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
